WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.227 --> 00:00:06.310
EMILY RAPP: Hey, everyone.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:09.860
Welcome to "Analytics
in a Multi-Cloud World."

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:14.370
I'm Emily Rapp, product
manager at BigQuery.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:18.390
We are facing a data explosion.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:24.240
175 zettabytes--
that's 21 zeros--

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.680
21 zeros of data in
the world by 2025.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.610
Where's it coming from?

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:35.170
We have cat videos,
server logs, monitoring.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.930
There's retail data.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.520
When you're thinking
about e-commerce,

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.010
it's a lot more than just
that final transaction.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.340
It's every point
on the sales cycle.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.010
IoT, manufacturing,
even my washing machine

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.810
is connected to the internet,
and therefore generating data.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:57.290
We have all of this
data at our fingertips.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.230
But we just can't
quite get to it

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:04.140
because we're living in
this world of data silos.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.750
There's organizational
separation.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.020
IT departments are
often centralized.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.810
And they are separate from
the central data department,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.120
if there is one,
which are even then

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.730
further separated into
the business units.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.260
And those business
units also have

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.080
analysts, data scientists
working and trying

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.010
to understand problems.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.360
It's fragmented.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.840
You have data originating from
a bunch of different sources.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.840
It's all in different
formats with various levels

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.720
of processing.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.410
This means that our analysts,
our data scientists,

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:38.610
our users--

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.300
our users are using
too many tools,

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.487
have to get data
from too many places,

00:01:44.487 --> 00:01:45.945
and they're making
too many copies.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:51.490
Why is this happening?

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.150
A key component is shown in one
of the Gartner cloud adoption

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:56.210
surveys.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:00.310
81% of companies who
are using public clouds

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:03.510
are actually using more
than one cloud provider.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.290
And so that means that
multi-cloud management is

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:10.070
becoming-- actually,
it is a true challenge.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:15.470
IT leaders, they want to
be agile, flexible, secure.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:17.300
And analysts and
data scientists,

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.580
the ones that are actually
doing the day-to-day work

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.950
with that data,
they're just trying

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:26.210
to help their organization
make the right decisions.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:29.210
And even if organizations decide
to have a centralized data

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:32.630
approach, like we talked
about in the earlier slide,

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:35.090
there's still always going
to be separate business

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:38.450
units, subsidiaries, partners.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:43.600
Multi-cloud is and will be
the world we're working in.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.360
We need to solve for
these macro challenges.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.930
So today, we're
introducing BigQuery Omni,

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:54.220
a flexible, multi-cloud solution
that lets you analyze data

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.110
across clouds.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.350
You don't have to
leave BigQuery.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:03.430
It's your familiar
interface, same experience.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:06.280
But now, you can
actually reach out--

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.560
reach out across those silos.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.470
BigQuery Omni is now in alpha.

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:14.470
And what's really cool is
that it's powered by Anthos.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:18.080
We don't have to do that
moving and copying of data.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.510
SPEAKER: As a data analyst
in the marketing department,

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:23.420
BigQuery Omni enables me
to use historical sales

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.480
data in my upcoming
marketing campaigns.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.520
Using our orders
data, we're going

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.350
to validate the
hypothesis that we should

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.420
be focusing our marketing
efforts beyond just

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:33.770
food and beverage.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:37.010
Our first query uses the
HyperLogLog approximation

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:40.850
function to sort the
departments by number of orders.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.550
We are using our read-only
external connection,

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:47.950
Retail Read, to get the data
from the Orders External table.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:49.880
The query returns
the departments,

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.680
sorted by number of orders.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.550
And as we can see, all top 10
are either food or beverage.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:57.710
This validates our
hypothesis that we

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.500
should be focusing our marketing
efforts on this customer

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.850
segment, household, or personal
care product purchasers.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:06.770
Now we need to
build a list of IDs

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:09.350
using our customer data set.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.850
The customer data has
personal information,

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.710
including name and zip code.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:18.110
And we need that data to
stay within the AWS region.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:21.200
However, we do have a
de-identified user ID field

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.220
that we can use
in our campaigns.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.920
Our next query will build
our audience segment,

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:30.220
buyers of household or
personal care products.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.020
Since our campaigns
are regionalized,

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.650
we only want customers
from Oregon or Washington.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:39.010
Now, we have a list of IDs that
can be used in any marketing

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.960
activation channel.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.720
We want this data to be
accessible to our marketers,

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.470
not just the analysts.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.820
So I use our right connection
to export the query result

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.780
to the marketing folder
that they have access to.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.570
And that is how you
can use BigQuery

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.750
Omni for data-driven
marketing activation.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:00.880
EMILY RAPP: That was
pretty cool, wasn't it?

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.240
Let's talk about bringing
multi-cloud analytics

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.310
to your data.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.680
First, we have to
break down those silos,

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.350
get you to getting
insights faster.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:13.540
Tap into the power of BigQuery.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:16.630
It's cost efficient,
no moving or copying.

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:19.060
You just instantly
query, instantly

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:23.250
get your results with that
consistent experience.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.750
It doesn't matter if your
data is in Google Cloud, AWS,

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.690
or, coming soon, Azure.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.000
You can just use the
same standard SQL,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.430
the same BigQuery
familiar interface,

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:37.340
write queries, build dashboards.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:41.520
Because the reality is
data, it's not just numbers.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.180
It's not just bytes stored in
some data center somewhere.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.090
It's the way that we can
actually communicate.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:49.470
We can tell stories.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.440
We can answer questions.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.700
We want you to be able to have
the ability to actually work

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:57.680
across those data silos.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:59.690
That data that was
at your fingertips,

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:02.470
let's get closer to it.

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:05.860
And we're able to do that
because under the hood,

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.110
we use Anthos.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.480
We think about BigQuery.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.477
BigQuery is the separation
of storage and compute.

00:06:13.477 --> 00:06:14.310
What does that mean?

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.510
It means you can scale up
and say, hey, sometimes when

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:20.900
things are going crazy, you may
need a little bit more compute.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.790
In other cases, maybe now
you're gathering a bunch of data

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:26.010
and so you need storage.

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:29.490
We were thinking about how do we
solve this data silo question?

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:32.460
But the challenge was
we have to figure out

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.210
how to get compute to the
data, even if that data is

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:39.320
in another cloud.

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.240
So BigQuery Omni, we run
the compute clusters,

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.510
known as Dremel, on
Anthos clusters in AWS.

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:50.090
We have a secure
connection, because it's

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:52.400
really critical-- we
think of that single pane,

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:54.740
that the control
plane and the metadata

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:59.360
can remain on Google Cloud,
but only the query result

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.550
actually passes through
the BigQuery routers

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:05.410
and then our secure connection
to the next region, AWS

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:07.220
or Azure.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.890
That connection can also
be used when users choose

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.720
to bring the results back.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:15.260
But the key piece is
it's choice for you.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:19.930
You can bring them back or you
can do everything within AWS.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.280
BigQuery Omni is a
fully managed service.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:24.590
A question I hear
is, oh, so I have

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:27.230
to use my Anthos or my Compute?

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:29.750
Nope, we got that covered.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:31.640
We do need your storage.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:34.370
And it's really critical that
you maintain the access control

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:36.370
to that storage.

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:39.690
But with the compute
resources running locally,

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:41.910
all that's done
behind the scenes.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:46.230
And you can just
answer those questions.

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:50.360
So how do we actually solve
some of those business scenarios

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:53.430
with BigQuery Omni?

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:54.270
This one's funny.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:55.920
This is my old world.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.740
Up until recently, in September,
when I joined BigQuery,

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:04.260
I was the infamous marketing
analytics adds measurement.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:08.700
Specifically, how
many people saw my ad?

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:11.610
It's a surprising hard question.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:14.630
And actually, it's even harder
to actually think about OK,

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:18.420
now I figured out what's going
on in my marketing campaigns.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:22.200
What about my audience
behavior in my apps on my site?

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.540
What impact did those
two have on my sales?

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.150
So let's think about how
could we solve that problem?

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:31.850
How could we see the impact?

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:33.450
We could visualize.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:34.880
So use BigQuery.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:39.200
Query Google Analytics 360
ads data in Google Cloud.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.280
And then tap into your
e-commerce data and app data

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:45.540
that's on AWS S3.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:48.410
Now with Looker, you
can build a dashboard,

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:52.430
so you or those executives
that we all have to worry about

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:55.160
can actually look at what
the audience behavior is

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.920
and purchases alongside spend.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:01.710
It's pretty amazing what seeing
something visually can do.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:03.445
It's like oh wait,
there's a spike.

00:09:03.445 --> 00:09:04.820
And then you also
talk to people.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:06.410
It's like no, really?

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:08.690
Building a dashboard when
data comes from all of those

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:11.400
different places,
and more importantly,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.210
making sure that if you share
that dashboard with someone,

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:16.890
they're not going to see
something that they shouldn't--

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:20.290
it's surprisingly challenging.

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:22.250
Now let's get a
little bit deeper.

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.280
This is something I'm
sure probably many of you

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:28.840
are familiar with, the
OK, my business team

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:31.330
said you need to use
the app data that we

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:32.960
have to do smart things.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.660
And we need that data.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:36.220
And we need that data here.

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.360
And it needs to be formatted
and easy to get to and fast.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:43.180
So as a data engineer, you
end up running thousands

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:46.900
of daily jobs to process logs
and build cross-cloud ETL

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:48.510
pipelines.

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.980
And what happens if
those pipelines go down?

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:56.050
So I've actually seen it where
instead of fixing the pipeline,

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:57.610
companies will
sometimes just hire

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:59.670
consultants to respond
to tickets to answer

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:00.970
the questions.

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:04.000
It's that complicated.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.110
With BigQuery Omni,
it's not always

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:10.590
just about getting new
things with dashboards.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:12.330
It's looking at your
existing workloads

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:16.250
and how you can optimize them.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.020
You have those petabytes
of click stream

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:19.860
logs that we talked about.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:22.100
And for some reason,
every app always

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:26.830
ends up going into different
S3 buckets with parquet files.

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:28.630
Today, you have those
thousands of jobs

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:30.510
that we talked about running.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.340
And all you're trying to do is
build audience lists and CSV

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:36.330
files of your active users.

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:41.050
Replace that with a single SQL
query that any analyst can run.

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:44.340
So now that any analyst or
data scientists can just

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:48.600
run a SQL query, let's
think about how that data

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:52.860
scientist can actually use the
data from sales to not just

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:54.600
understand their
existing audience,

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.310
but reach new audiences
more effectively.

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:00.290
SPEAKER: Let's get started
with BigQuery Omni.

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:02.730
In the BigQuery UI
on Google Cloud,

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:05.490
choose the public cloud region
where your data is located

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:07.590
and run your query,
the same query

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:11.400
you would use to work with
data that's stored on GCP.

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:14.100
The query is passed to
the other public cloud.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:17.040
And all of the compute is
done within that public cloud

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.390
region.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:21.180
The results are returned
to the BigQuery UI.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.060
Or you can choose to export
the results directly back

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:27.000
to your data storage on
that public cloud, which

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.080
means there is no cross-cloud
movement of results or data.

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:34.730
You can start using
BigQuery Omni right away.

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:37.940
There's no need to do any
formatting or transformation

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:39.150
of your data.

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:43.190
BigQuery Omni supports formats
such as AVRO, CSV, JSON,

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:45.440
ORC, and parquet.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:48.440
You also don't need to move
or copy your raw data out

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:51.320
of the other public cloud,
do any cluster management,

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:53.360
or provision any resources.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.700
All computation occurs
within BigQuery's

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:58.640
multi-tenant service, which
is running on the region

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.740
where the data is
currently located.

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:03.080
Behind the scenes,
BigQuery's query engine

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:04.910
is running on our
Anthos clusters

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:07.340
within the BigQuery
managed service.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:10.160
We get the data from data
storage within your account

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.020
only once you've
authorized permissions

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:15.360
via your other public
clouds' IAM roles.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:18.230
Those roles are tied to a
BigQuery Omni connection

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:19.260
object.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:22.950
You can change, grant, or
revoke access at anytime.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:25.880
And the connection object can
be shared to multiple users

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:27.890
within that same project.

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:31.250
We maintain a secure connection
to bring your query results

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:33.910
back to Google Cloud.

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.590
Welcome to BigQuery Omni.

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:40.180
EMILY RAPP: OK, so
we did that query.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:43.000
We exported it back to AWS.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.220
And now it's time to activate
your cross-cloud data

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:48.490
story, which means think
about that Google Analytics

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:50.260
data that's coming in.

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:52.630
Think about the S3 data storage.

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:54.820
And take those audience
lists and do things

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:57.230
like look-like targeting.

00:12:57.230 --> 00:13:00.290
Ever had that experience
where you buy something

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:04.580
and you still see the ad
over and over and over again?

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:07.490
And you're like come on, I
bought that thing already.

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:10.750
Stop wasting your ads on me.

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:13.390
Now, we can actually
solve that, because we

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:16.600
can tie that commerce
data to the ad platform

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.380
in a safe and secure way.

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:22.300
So let's recap.

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:25.300
Embrace a flexible
multi-cloud analytics solution

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:27.920
with BigQuery Omni.

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:30.470
We're going to break
down silos, get straight

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:33.610
to the getting insights
part of data analytics.

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:36.240
It's the fun part, anyways.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.420
Having a consistent
experience across clouds--

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:42.090
it doesn't matter where
your data sets are.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:44.880
You should be able to use
the standard SQL BigQuery

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:48.750
interface, write your queries,
build your dashboards.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:52.530
It's all about taking that
consistency and familiarity

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:55.350
to accelerate the
time to insights.

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:57.870
You want to be able to
wake up in the morning,

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.540
check your phone, be
like, OK, we're good,

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:03.510
and go back to sleep, not
have to worry about writing

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:05.970
a whole ton of different
queries in different tools

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:09.220
before you know the
state of your business.

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:12.750
And all this is possible
because of Anthos.

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:14.940
Dremel was built by Google.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:17.510
Dremel is what powers BigQuery.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:20.320
Anthos-- it's that next stage.

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:22.720
Thinking about
that Gartner quote,

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:26.740
81% of companies-- it's
probably, actually, more

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:29.410
than that, I would say, maybe.

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:32.770
Think about everyone has
some part of their business

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:35.270
that's on some other cloud.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:37.730
And so by bringing
the data to you,

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:40.940
we bring the compute
to where your data is.

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:42.560
We worry about the
infrastructure.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:45.830
Don't worry about spinning
up clusters, managing them.

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:47.060
We got that.

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:49.670
You focus on driving
the business,

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:52.220
doing some really
cool data science,

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:55.130
making the decisions
you need with data,

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:58.250
not worrying about
how do I set this up

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:02.270
and what pipeline should I
run, and oh, wait, they broke.

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:05.030
Now I have to hire a
whole bunch of people

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:08.450
to just answer tickets.

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:09.700
Let's get started.

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:11.870
Get started with BigQuery Omni.

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:13.820
Let's take it to the next level.

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.730
Take your multi-cloud
analytic strategy

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:18.640
that you've built from a
platform and IT perspective

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:20.490
and bring it to your data.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:22.730
BigQuery Omni is in alpha.

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:26.670
So if you're interested,
please go to the link

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:29.540
